{
  "$schema": "https://turbo.build/schema.json",
  "tasks": {
    // build — NOT CACHED, on the same reasoning `test` already carries.
    //
    // tsup emits CONTENT-HASHED chunks and relies on `clean: true` to remove the
    // previous generation. A turbo cache HIT skips tsup entirely, so `clean`
    // never runs and restored artifacts land on top of whatever generation was
    // already on disk. The result is a dist that mixes two builds: a surviving
    // `chunk-A.js` importing `./chunk-B.js` that the restore did not bring back.
    //
    // It failed as "Failed to load url ./chunk-XXXX.js" from vitest, on roughly
    // one run in five, on identical input — the worst failure shape there is,
    // because four green runs read as proof.
    //
    // Declaring narrower `outputs` does not fix it; the defect is that a hit
    // SKIPS the cleaning step, not that the outputs are mis-declared. The build
    // is ~4s. This repository already spent that argument on `test` and reached
    // the same answer: correctness is worth the seconds in a project whose whole
    // thesis is verification.
    "build": {
      "dependsOn": ["^build"],
      "outputs": ["dist/**"],
      "cache": false
    },
    "typecheck": {
      "dependsOn": ["^build"]
    },
    // `build` — the package's OWN — and not just `^build`.
    //
    // A test tree may reference its own package through its PUBLIC specifier
    // rather than by relative path: `canon/test/runtime-shim.test.ts` drives the
    // shipped `cratylus project` over the repo's `cratylus.config.ts`, and that
    // config extends `@cratylus/canon` — the PACKAGE, i.e. this package's `dist`.
    // `^build` builds a package's DEPENDENCIES and never the package itself, so on
    // a cold tree `tsc` ran against a `dist` that did not exist yet.
    //
    // This surfaced the moment `typecheck:test` became a dependency of `test`:
    // canon's `test` script had always carried a manual `pnpm run build &&` for
    // exactly this reason, and the type-check inherited the requirement without
    // the workaround. Declaring it here is the fix rather than copying the prefix,
    // because the dependency is a fact about the task graph and belongs in it.
    "typecheck:test": {
      "dependsOn": ["^build", "build"]
    },
    // test — NOT CACHED, deliberately.
    //
    // Five canon gates scan by `git ls-files` from the repo ROOT: architecture,
    // command-veracity, hook-rule-boundary, bin-name-single-home and
    // gate-convicts reach AGENTS.md, the root ground docs, and other packages'
    // manifests. Turbo's cache key is a package's own inputs plus its
    // dependencies' outputs — provably NARROWER than that scan domain. A commit
    // touching only the scanned corpus produces a HIT and REPLAYS the previous
    // green log without running the gate, so cached-green becomes
    // indistinguishable from actually-green. That is precisely the defect
    // `gate-convicts.test.ts` exists to forbid one level down.
    //
    // Declaring the true `inputs` is the alternative and it is worse: the honest
    // input set here is "everything git tracks", which is a cache that never hits
    // wearing the costume of one that does.
    //
    // The suite is ~11s. Correctness is worth 11 seconds in a repository whose
    // entire thesis is verification.
    // `typecheck:test` is a DEPENDENCY of `test`, not a sibling task.
    //
    // It went red on a clean tree and stayed red, because `pnpm test` ran a
    // 9-task pipeline that did not include it: the repository's green suite and
    // its red type-check were two different commands, and only one was in
    // anyone's habit. That is worse than an absent check — it is an UNWATCHED
    // one, and the tests are the enforcement layer for every naming and shape
    // guarantee this project makes. An unchecked test tree is an unchecked
    // enforcement layer.
    //
    // Declaring it here rather than in a release script is deliberate: a gate
    // that only the release runs is a gate that fails at the worst moment.
    "test": {
      "dependsOn": ["^build", "typecheck:test"],
      "cache": false
    },
    // canon's suite drives the BUILT `cratylus` bin, a package it does not depend on.
    //
    // `version-single-home.test.ts` execs `packages/cli/dist/cratylus.js --version`,
    // because a version is a claim about the ARTIFACT and only the emitted bin can be
    // asked it. `deploy-drift-notice.test.ts` points its fixture shims at the same
    // file, so the worker under test runs the real command rather than a mock. canon
    // depends on forge, runtime and schema, not on `cratylus`, so `^build` never
    // builds that file: on a cold tree it does not exist, and a suite that passes
    // only because a previous run left `dist` behind is not passing.
    //
    // Declared as an explicit cross-package task edge rather than by adding a
    // devDependency: the fact is "this suite needs that artifact built", which is a
    // statement about the TASK GRAPH. Making it a package dependency would put a
    // canon → cratylus edge in the manifest to satisfy a test fixture, which is a
    // worse lie than the one it fixes.
    "@cratylus/canon#test": {
      "dependsOn": ["^build", "build", "typecheck:test", "cratylus#build"],
      "cache": false
    },
    // forge's suite drives the SAME built bin, and has the same gap.
    //
    // `integrate-smoke.test.ts` materializes the `cratylus` package with `pnpm deploy`
    // — which copies its `dist`, so the bin must already be built — then deploys a
    // thin shim and RUNS it, and the shim forwards to `cratylus <capability>` off
    // PATH. The whole point of the gate is that the bin is the real one. forge does
    // not depend on the `cratylus` package, so without this edge nothing orders that
    // build against this suite: it passes alone on a `dist` a prior build left
    // behind, and fails in the pipeline when the bin is rebuilt underneath a test
    // already spawning it. Green when run alone, red in the pipeline, on identical
    // input: the shape this file already refuses one entry up.
    "@cratylus/forge#test": {
      "dependsOn": ["^build", "typecheck:test", "cratylus#build"],
      "cache": false
    },
    // the cli's suite spawns its OWN bin, so its test task names its own build.
    //
    // `closed-pipe.test.ts` runs `packages/cli/dist/cratylus.js` with its stdout on a
    // pipe it closes after the first line: that a reader which closes the pipe ends the
    // command quietly is a fact about the emitted process, which the in-process program
    // `command-tree.test.ts` builds cannot be asked. The generic `test` task reaches
    // the package's own `build` only through `typecheck:test`, so a suite that ran
    // before that edge changed, or a task graph that reordered it, would run against a
    // `dist` a previous build left behind. Declared here, the way forge's and canon's
    // are, because the dependency is a fact about the task graph.
    "cratylus#test": {
      "dependsOn": ["^build", "build", "typecheck:test"],
      "cache": false
    },
    "dev": {
      "cache": false,
      "persistent": true,
      "dependsOn": ["^build"]
    }
  }
}
